## Proof that $HJ(2, r) = r$

**Setup.** We work with $[2]^n = \{1,2\}^n$. Via the bijection $1 \leftrightarrow 0,\; 2 \leftrightarrow 1$, we identify $[2]^n$ with the Boolean lattice $\{0,1\}^n \cong 2^{[n]}$ (the power set of $[n]$, ordered by inclusion $\subseteq$).

**Combinatorial lines for $k=2$.** A combinatorial line in $[2]^n$ is determined by a word $w \in \{1, 2, *\}^n$ with at least one $*$, and consists of the two points $w|_{*=1}$ and $w|_{*=2}$. Under our identification, these correspond to two subsets $A, B \subseteq [n]$ where $A \subseteq B$ and $A \neq B$ (the $*$-coordinates become elements of $B \setminus A$). Conversely, any pair $A \subsetneq B$ of subsets determines a combinatorial line (place $*$ on coordinates in $B \setminus A$, fix the rest).

Therefore, **a monochromatic combinatorial line in $[2]^n$ is exactly a pair of comparable distinct elements $A \subsetneq B$ in the Boolean lattice $2^{[n]}$ receiving the same color.**

---

**Lower bound: $HJ(2, r) \geq r$.**

We exhibit an $r$-coloring of $[2]^{r-1}$ with no monochromatic combinatorial line. Color each subset $S \subseteq [r-1]$ by its cardinality $|S| \in \{0, 1, \ldots, r-1\}$, using all $r$ colors. If $A \subsetneq B$, then $|A| < |B|$, so $A$ and $B$ receive distinct colors. Hence no monochromatic combinatorial line exists, giving $HJ(2, r) > r - 1$, i.e., $HJ(2, r) \geq r$.

---

**Upper bound: $HJ(2, r) \leq r$.**

We show every $r$-coloring of $[2]^r$ contains a monochromatic combinatorial line. The Boolean lattice $2^{[r]}$ contains a maximal chain of length $r + 1$:
$$\emptyset \;\subset\; \{1\} \;\subset\; \{1,2\} \;\subset\; \cdots \;\subset\; [r].$$
This chain has $r + 1$ elements. By the **pigeonhole principle**, since only $r$ colors are used, two distinct elements of this chain share a color. Being in a chain, they are comparable with one strictly contained in the other, hence they form a monochromatic combinatorial line.

*(Equivalently, by Mirsky's theorem: the minimum number of antichains needed to partition a finite poset equals its longest chain length. Since $2^{[r]}$ has a longest chain of length $r+1$, it cannot be partitioned into $r$ antichains. Thus any $r$-coloring has a color class that is not an antichain, containing a comparable pair — a monochromatic line.)*

---

**Conclusion.** Combining $HJ(2, r) \geq r$ and $HJ(2, r) \leq r$:

$$\boxed{HJ(2, r) = r.}$$

